WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.540
Consider Care4U, an AI system designed to
detect early signs of patient

2
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.100
deterioration in a hospital setting,
aiming to reduce unnecessary ICU

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.980
admissions. The architecture relies on
continuous biometric data streamed from

4
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.480
wearable sensors. This live feed is
evaluated against a neural network trained

5
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.340
on 50,000 historical hospital cases.

6
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.700
The model continuously monitors vitals,
pushing automated medical alerts

7
00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.940
directly to the nursing staff's mobile
devices.

8
00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.100
Operating within the European Union, a
tool like this falls under the EU-AI

9
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.140
Act. Because it makes medical determinations,
it triggers strict regulatory

10
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.340
classifications for high-risk AI
applications.

11
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.680
Deploying an algorithm in a live clinical
environment means the stakes are

12
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.020
literal life and death. The ethical
scrutiny applied by the committee must

13
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.240
scale to match that level of risk.

14
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.400
This checklist divides our evaluation of
the Care-for-You system into two

15
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.040
distinct dimensions. human and social,
alongside technical and legal.

16
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.040
Under the human dimension, we must
evaluate automation bias.

17
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.520
If the system is highly efficient, nurses
may begin to rely too heavily on the

18
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.020
automated alerts, eroding their
independent clinical judgment.

19
00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.160
We also have to interrogate the data set
itself.

20
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.920
50,000 cases is a large number, but if
those historical files under-represent

21
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.940
women, older adults, or minority groups,
the system will perform worse for

22
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.200
them in real time. We must also factor in
the psychological impact.

23
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.200
Continuous algorithmic monitoring can
severely increase patient anxiety and

24
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.800
shift the fundamental dynamic of the
doctor-patient relationship.

25
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.020
Shifting to the legal dimension, we look
at consent.

26
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.800
Patients must be clearly informed about
the real-time data collection,

27
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.620
and they must have the ability to opt out
of the AI monitoring without

28
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.620
compromising their standard of care.

29
00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.960
We return to the black box problem.

30
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.200
If a clinician cannot look at an alert and
understand exactly why the system

31
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.520
issued it, they are forced to either
blindly trust a machine or ignore the

32
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.700
warning entirely. And finally,
accountability.

33
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:10.180
If the system misses a critical
deterioration event or issues a false alert

34
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:14.260
that leads to the wrong treatment, the
proposal must clearly define who holds

35
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.380
the legal and ethical responsibility.

36
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:21.080
An AI tool deployed in a high stakes
environment is only as robust as its

37
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.420
weakest technical, legal, or ethical link.

38
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.400
Both of these cases demonstrate a core
principle for Essex committees.
